 📘 Recursion in Java – Study Notes

 🔁 1. What is Recursion?
Definition: A method calling itself to solve smaller instances of the same problem.
 Structure:
void recurse(parameters) {
    if (base_condition) return;         // Base case
    recurse(smaller_problem);           // Recursive case
}

 🧠 2. Key Components of Recursion
| Component      | Description                                              |
|  |  |
| Base Case      | Stops recursion; prevents infinite loop                  |
| Recursive Case | Reduces the problem and makes the recursive call         |
| Call Stack     | Each recursive call is stored in memory until it returns |

 🧩 3. Types of Recursion
| Type     | Description                              | Example                     |
|  |  |  |
| Direct   | Method calls itself directly             | f() { f(); }              |
| Indirect | Method A calls B, B calls A              | a() { b(); } b() { a(); } |
| Tail     | Recursive call is the last statement     | return recurse(n1);      |
| NonTail | Recursive call followed by extra work    | int x = recurse(n1) + 1; |
| Tree     | Multiple recursive calls in one function | f(n) = f(n1) + f(n2)    |

 📏 4. Designing a Recursive Function
1. Define the problem in terms of smaller subproblems.
2. Identify the base case.
3. Determine how to reduce the problem toward the base case.
4. Make the recursive call.
5. Combine results (if needed).

 ⚠️ 5. Common Mistakes to Avoid
 Forgetting the base case → StackOverflowError
 Not reducing the problem → Infinite recursion
 Using recursion for large n without optimization (memoization)

 💻 6. JavaSpecific Notes
 Recursion uses the call stack internally.
 Java does not optimize tail recursion → avoid deep recursions.
 Prefer iteration or memoization for heavy recursion.

 ⏱️ 7. Time and Space Complexity
| Pattern          | Time Complexity | Space Complexity (stack) |
|  |  |  |
| Linear           | O(n)            | O(n)                     |
| Binary Tree      | O(2^n)          | O(n)                     |
| With Memoization | O(n) or O(n^2)  | O(n)                     |

 🔍 8. Common Recursive Patterns
| Pattern              | Used For                              |
|  |  |
| Linear Recursion     | Factorial, Sum, Fibonacci (bad)       |
| Divide & Conquer     | Merge Sort, Binary Search             |
| Backtracking         | NQueens, Maze, Subsets, Permutations |
| Recursion with State | Tree Traversals, Graph DFS            |
| Memoization          | Fibonacci, DP problems                |

 🌳 9. Understanding via Recursion Trees
Use a recursion tree to:
 Visualize function calls
 Analyze time complexity
 Understand depth of recursion

 🧰 10. Tips for Interview Preparation
 Always start with base case when writing code.
 Dryrun the code for small inputs.
 Think in terms of problem reduction.
 Recognize patterns: Backtracking, Divide & Conquer.
 Be able to convert recursion to iteration (and vice versa).
 Practice writing both recursive and iterative solutions.
 Explain your recursion tree to the interviewer if needed.
